# The "## Behavior" section of every library page in docs/: the one source of its words. tests/test_docs_pages.py renders
# it for each page that has the section, and fails when the page's copy differs, printing the section expected.
#
# A line is CONDITIONS | TEXT. A line is in a page's section when all its conditions hold:
#   all          every page
#   py, ts       the page has Python examples; it has TypeScript ones
#   py-only, ts-only, both   only Python, only TypeScript, both
#   NAME.md      that page;  !NAME.md  every page but that one
# In TEXT, {provider} is the page's provider, from the `provider` lines below ("your provider" for the rest), and {key}
# is "your {provider} key", or "your provider key" where the provider is "your provider".
#
# A page's own case is a line here, conditioned on that page: change the words here, then copy the section the test prints.

provider openai.md OpenAI
provider anthropic.md Anthropic
provider gemini.md Gemini
provider bedrock.md Bedrock

all | ## Behavior
all |
both | - **Reflexer down, slow or busy:** the SDK sends the call directly to {provider}, and your code gets {provider}'s answer. The SDK logs a warning (Python: on the `reflexer` logger; TypeScript: with `console.warn`). After a failure, the SDK sends the next calls directly to {provider} too, for about a minute at most. Then the SDK tries reflexer again.
py-only | - **Reflexer down, slow or busy:** the SDK sends the call directly to {provider}, and your code gets {provider}'s answer. The SDK logs a warning on the `reflexer` logger. After a failure, the SDK sends the next calls directly to {provider} too, for about a minute at most. Then the SDK tries reflexer again.
ts-only | - **Reflexer down, slow or busy:** the SDK sends the call directly to {provider}, and your code gets {provider}'s answer. The SDK logs a warning with `console.warn`. After a failure, the SDK sends the next calls directly to {provider} too, for about a minute at most. Then the SDK tries reflexer again.
py | - **Timeouts, Python:** your client's timeout applies to the call to reflexer. If that timeout expires, the SDK sends the call directly to {provider}, with the same timeout. So one call can take up to two times your timeout. If reflexer had already forwarded the call, {provider} may run the call twice and bill it twice.
ts !bedrock.md | - **Timeouts, TypeScript:** if reflexer's answer has not started after `headersTimeout` (300 seconds by default), the SDK sends the call directly to {provider}. If your client's own timeout expires first, that attempt fails with a timeout error. If your provider SDK then retries the call, the SDK sends the retry directly to {provider}. In every case, if reflexer had already forwarded the call, {provider} may run the call twice and bill it twice.
bedrock.md | - **Timeouts, TypeScript:** if reflexer's answer has not started after `headersTimeout` (300 seconds by default), the SDK sends the call directly to Bedrock. If your client's own timeout expires first, the result depends on the library. AnthropicBedrock and the AI SDK (not streamed) send the call to Bedrock again, with the same timeout, so one call can take up to two times your timeout. The AI SDK (streamed) fails with a timeout error. In every case, if reflexer had already forwarded the call, Bedrock may run the call twice and bill it twice.
all | - **Key refused:** if reflexer refuses your reflexer key, your provider SDK raises its authentication error, and the SDK does not send the call to {provider}. If your reflexer key was revoked less than one day ago, the SDK still sends your calls directly to {provider}, and logs a warning.
all | - **Which calls:** the SDK sends to reflexer only the calls that return a model's message. The SDK sends all other calls, and all calls to `http`, `localhost` or a private IP, to the address your code gave them, as before.
all | - **What leaves your machine:** the SDK sends the call to reflexer, with {key}. When reflexer does not answer the call itself, reflexer uses this key to forward the call to {provider}. Only reflexer receives your reflexer key.
gemini.md | - **No timeout by default, Python:** google-genai sets no timeout, so no timeout of yours limits how long a call waits for reflexer. Set one in `http_options={"timeout": 60_000}` (milliseconds).
langchain.md | - **Gemini, no timeout by default:** google-genai sets no timeout, so no timeout of yours limits how long a call waits for reflexer. Give `ChatGoogleGenerativeAI` a `timeout=`.
langchain.md | - **Not covered:** the calls of `ChatAnthropic` (Python and LangChain.js) and `@langchain/google-genai` cannot go through reflexer: [Known limits](limits.md).
pydantic-ai.md | - **Gemini, no timeout by default:** google-genai sets no timeout, so no timeout of yours limits how long a call waits for reflexer. Give `reflexer.gemini.Client` the argument `http_options={"timeout": 60_000}` (milliseconds).
bedrock.md | - **A signed call:** the SDK sends the call to reflexer exactly as your library signed it: the call, its signature, your access key ID and, with temporary credentials, your session token. The SDK never sends your AWS secret: only your library's signer reads it. Reflexer never forwards your signature. If you gave reflexer a role in your AWS account at onboarding, reflexer can forward the call to Bedrock itself. Otherwise, when reflexer does not answer a call, the SDK sends the call directly to Bedrock, as when reflexer is down, and your library signs the call again. LiteLLM and the AI SDK do not sign the call again: the SDK sends it with its first signature, while that signature is still valid ([Known limits](limits.md)).
bedrock.md | - **Mantle:** Python only. With a Bedrock API key, the SDK sends Mantle calls to reflexer. The SDK sends signed Mantle calls directly to Bedrock.
litellm.md | - **Your whole process:** the line sets LiteLLM's own settings, so the SDK sends every LiteLLM call of your process through reflexer. If your code sets `client_session`, `aclient_session` or `sync_transport` after the line, or replaces the list with `litellm.callbacks = [...]`, the SDK no longer sees the calls that setting covers, and LiteLLM sends them directly to your provider: [Known limits](limits.md).
litellm.md | - **Gemini 3:** LiteLLM sends Gemini 3 calls to `v1alpha`, which reflexer does not take. The SDK sends those calls directly to Gemini.
!other-libraries.md | - **What stays the same:** your arguments, the answer's types and fields, errors and streams. If you remove the line, your code works as before.
other-libraries.md | - **What stays the same:** your arguments, the answer's types and fields, errors and streams. If you remove the reflexer client, your code works as before.
all |
all | Every case, with what to do: [When reflexer fails](when-reflexer-fails.md). Every limit: [Known limits](limits.md).
